  "common_analysis_conditions": {
    "inferential_unit": "donor; cells and pooled libraries are not independent replicates",
    "primary_design": "Fit each cohort separately using strict young <=40 versus old >60 groups. GSE214546 young means the 16 young adults, not pediatric donors. Exact-age continuous models and age>=60 atlas-reproduction models are separate sensitivity analyses, not substitutes for the strict replication contrast.",
    "eligibility": "Require at least 20 released-annotation cells per donor-cell type, at least 3 donors per age group and at least 6 scored donors per fitted cell type, with residual degrees of freedom after covariates. P1 additionally requires detected up- and down-program genes. Hellinger requires at least two young-reference donors, >=50 cross-fitted young-reference cells and >=20 passing cells per scored donor.",
    "covariates": "Include sex when identifiable. For GSE214546 also evaluate released CMV status and pool/batch identifiability; do not retain unidentifiable covariates. Depth adjustment is a labeled sensitivity for P1/CLR and part of the primary Hellinger model where identifiable.",
    "multiplicity": "BH within each cohort and prespecified endpoint/model family. Do not pool p-values across cohorts or treat cells as replication.",
    "replication": "Compare harmonized donor-level effect sizes and 95% confidence intervals in forest plots, with sign/direction and interval summaries. Confirmatory composition targets are CD8-naive T-cell decrease, MAIT decrease and non-classical-monocyte increase; confirmatory CD8-naive pseudobulk targets are PTGER2, CLIC1, S100A4, PRF1, GZMA and CCL5 increases. Other common cell types/genes are exploratory and FDR-controlled. Unmatched labels are reported untestable, never forced together.",
    "resources": "Acquire and process one cohort at a time under scripts/resource_guard.py with 30 GB disk reserve, per-file caps and <=8 GB analysis memory. Prefer per-donor files; never use the 32,088,821,760-byte GSE214546 RAW tar for this plan."
  },
